Kind: captions
Language: en

NOTE Module 2: The Empirical Loop narrated walkthrough. Generated from the
narration script and per-segment audio durations (0.7 s gap per slide).

s01-001
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.037
Welcome back.

s01-002
00:00:01.087 --> 00:00:04.570
This is Module 2 of Claude Code
for Accounting Research,

s01-003
00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:09.930
the empirical loop on the SEC's Financial
Statement Data Sets. I'm John Barrios.

s01-004
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:14.747
So in this module we take one accounting
question from a vague idea all the way

s01-005
00:00:14.797 --> 00:00:16.110
to a verified figure.

s02-006
00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.682
Here's how the live session runs.

s02-007
00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:22.027
About forty minutes of lecture
on the dataset, the loop,

s02-008
00:00:22.077 --> 00:00:24.405
and the habits that keep
the work reproducible.

s02-009
00:00:24.455 --> 00:00:29.002
Then a thirty-five minute demo,
where I build R&D intensity by industry,

s02-010
00:00:29.052 --> 00:00:32.077
starting from an empty folder
and ending with a figure.

s02-011
00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:33.419
A ten-minute break.

s02-012
00:00:33.469 --> 00:00:37.522
Then an eighty-minute lab where you do
the same thing with your own measure.

s02-013
00:00:37.572 --> 00:00:41.897
And we close with a debrief,
the exit ticket, and the WRDS checkpoint,

s02-014
00:00:41.947 --> 00:00:43.535
which is a hard gate.

s02-015
00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:47.290
This video covers the lecture slides
and previews the demo and the lab.

s03-016
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:53.460
So Module 1 gave you three habits: files
persist, trust but verify, and be specific.

s03-017
00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:57.971
But you only used them on
one script and one number.

s03-018
00:00:58.021 --> 00:01:01.601
In this module we take one accounting
question through the full pipeline,

s03-019
00:01:01.651 --> 00:01:03.031
end to end.

s03-020
00:01:03.081 --> 00:01:04.986
The diagram is that loop.

s03-021
00:01:05.036 --> 00:01:09.155
A vague idea becomes a precise task,
then scripts, data, a figure, and a README,

s03-022
00:01:09.205 --> 00:01:13.193
and the verify step at the end
leads you to the next question.

s03-023
00:01:13.243 --> 00:01:16.579
Every one of those stages
ends up as a file on disk,

s03-024
00:01:16.629 --> 00:01:19.510
so a later session can
pick it up from there.

s04-025
00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:25.882
The data for this module is the SEC's
Financial Statement Data Sets, FSDS.

s04-026
00:01:25.932 --> 00:01:28.164
It comes as one zip file
per quarter, it's free,

s04-027
00:01:28.214 --> 00:01:32.926
and you don't need a WRDS
account or any other credentials.

s04-028
00:01:32.976 --> 00:01:35.579
Two files inside each zip matter.

s04-029
00:01:35.629 --> 00:01:41.933
sub.txt has one row per filing:
the accession number, called ADSH, plus the CIK,

s04-030
00:01:41.983 --> 00:01:46.003
the SIC code, the form
type, and the period.

s04-031
00:01:46.053 --> 00:01:49.435
num.txt has one row per tagged value:
the ADSH, the XBRL tag, the date,

s04-032
00:01:49.485 --> 00:01:52.934
the number of quarters, and the value.

s04-033
00:01:52.984 --> 00:01:56.931
You join the two on ADSH,
and every time you join,

s04-034
00:01:56.981 --> 00:02:00.035
you state the row counts before and after.

s04-035
00:02:00.085 --> 00:02:05.650
Compustat has the same shape, which is
why we learn it here before Module 4.

s05-036
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:11.005
Before the first figure, have
Claude write a DATA_PROFILE.md.

s05-037
00:02:11.055 --> 00:02:14.789
It's a short file that says what one row
of each table is and what can go wrong

s05-038
00:02:14.839 --> 00:02:16.037
with it.

s05-039
00:02:16.087 --> 00:02:19.490
You ask for it in the opening prompt,
next to DECISIONS.md and LOG.md,

s05-040
00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:22.504
so it gets written before
any measure is computed,

s05-041
00:02:22.554 --> 00:02:24.674
and every later session can read it.

s05-042
00:02:24.724 --> 00:02:29.435
That's where you catch the errors you
wouldn't see in a clean-looking figure.

s05-043
00:02:29.485 --> 00:02:33.445
This is the files-persist habit
from Module 1, applied to the data.

s05-044
00:02:33.495 --> 00:02:36.910
And in Module 4 you'll ask the
same questions of Compustat.

s06-045
00:02:37.660 --> 00:02:41.239
For FSDS, the profile
answers four questions.

s06-046
00:02:41.289 --> 00:02:42.352
First, what is one row?

s06-047
00:02:42.402 --> 00:02:43.510
In sub.txt it's one filing.

s06-048
00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:49.244
In num.txt it's one value: a tag, for a date,
over a number of quarters, in a unit,

s06-049
00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.139
within a filing.

s06-050
00:02:51.189 --> 00:02:53.022
Second, is that row unique?

s06-051
00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:56.767
Have Claude look up the key
columns in the SEC's documentation,

s06-052
00:02:56.817 --> 00:03:02.296
then count the rows and the distinct
key combinations and report both.

s06-053
00:03:02.346 --> 00:03:08.154
Third, tag coverage: the share of 10-Ks in
each pinned quarter that report each tag.

s06-054
00:03:08.204 --> 00:03:10.511
If a third of filers never report a tag,

s06-055
00:03:10.561 --> 00:03:14.500
your measure partly reflects
which firms report it.

s06-056
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:15.907
Fourth, known problems.

s06-057
00:03:15.957 --> 00:03:19.730
A 10-K shows the prior year
next to the current one,

s06-058
00:03:19.780 --> 00:03:24.332
so the same firm-year value
appears in more than one filing,

s06-059
00:03:24.382 --> 00:03:27.583
and 10-K/A amendments
restate earlier values.

s06-060
00:03:27.633 --> 00:03:30.330
For each one, write down
which filing you keep.

s07-061
00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:36.622
Here's the question for the demo: how has
R&D intensity evolved across industries?

s07-062
00:03:36.672 --> 00:03:38.783
It's a reasonable research question.

s07-063
00:03:38.833 --> 00:03:42.914
As a prompt, though, it doesn't say
which years, which industry grouping,

s07-064
00:03:42.964 --> 00:03:46.530
which measure of R&D, or what
the output should look like.

s07-065
00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:48.141
So the agent has to guess,

s07-066
00:03:48.191 --> 00:03:52.218
and you spend your first several turns
finding out which guesses were wrong.

s07-067
00:03:52.268 --> 00:03:57.142
Module 1's four components, file,
operation, aggregation, and output,

s07-068
00:03:57.192 --> 00:03:59.005
close most of that gap.

s07-069
00:03:59.055 --> 00:04:02.421
A pipeline with more than one
file adds two more decisions:

s07-070
00:04:02.471 --> 00:04:04.070
the join and the paper trail.

s08-071
00:04:04.820 --> 00:04:08.524
So before you type anything,
answer five questions.

s08-072
00:04:08.574 --> 00:04:13.864
Which files: which FSDS quarters,
and which two files inside each.

s08-073
00:04:13.914 --> 00:04:17.309
Which join: on what key,
and how you'll check it.

s08-074
00:04:17.359 --> 00:04:21.661
Which measure: R&D over total assets,
but which XBRL tags, and filtered how.

s08-075
00:04:21.711 --> 00:04:26.686
Which aggregation: what statistic,
grouped by what, over what time unit.

s08-076
00:04:26.736 --> 00:04:31.921
And which output: what figure, saved
under what name, alongside what table.

s08-077
00:04:31.971 --> 00:04:36.598
You're the domain expert, so you
make each of those calls yourself,

s08-078
00:04:36.648 --> 00:04:39.250
before the agent has to guess at them.

s09-079
00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.833
Three habits go in before
the opening prompt,

s09-080
00:04:42.883 --> 00:04:45.570
because adding them
later costs a lot more.

s09-081
00:04:45.620 --> 00:04:48.053
First, empty-folder discipline.

s09-082
00:04:48.103 --> 00:04:52.799
Every project starts in a fresh directory,
so you make a folder called lab 2,

s09-083
00:04:52.849 --> 00:04:55.202
move into it, and launch Claude there.

s09-084
00:04:55.252 --> 00:04:59.555
An agent that opens in a folder full
of last month's Stata do-files will

s09-085
00:04:59.605 --> 00:05:03.497
sometimes let those files shape
what it thinks the project is.

s09-086
00:05:03.547 --> 00:05:04.941
Second, named scripts.

s09-087
00:05:04.991 --> 00:05:08.717
Ask yourself, if I deleted the
whole conversation right now,

s09-088
00:05:08.767 --> 00:05:10.674
would the pipeline still run?

s09-089
00:05:10.724 --> 00:05:12.982
Third, paper-trail prompting.

s09-090
00:05:13.032 --> 00:05:15.764
You ask for the data profile,
DECISIONS.md, LOG.md,

s09-091
00:05:15.814 --> 00:05:19.800
and the attrition rows in the
same prompt that starts the work,

s09-092
00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:24.990
before any code runs, so the record gets
written while you're making the choices.

s10-093
00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.665
This is the opening prompt from the demo,

s10-094
00:05:28.715 --> 00:05:33.529
and it's the piece of this module
you'll reuse the most. It has six parts.

s10-095
00:05:33.579 --> 00:05:37.849
Data: the FSDS quarters,
sub and num, with the columns named.

s10-096
00:05:37.899 --> 00:05:44.196
In the demo that's the first-quarter file
for each year from 2011 through 2024,

s10-097
00:05:44.246 --> 00:05:47.962
which holds most 10-Ks for
fiscal 2010 through 2023.

s10-098
00:05:48.012 --> 00:05:51.796
Join: merge on ADSH, keep
only the consolidated rows,

s10-099
00:05:51.846 --> 00:05:57.423
state the row counts before and after,
and flag anything that doesn't match.

s10-100
00:05:57.473 --> 00:06:02.635
Measure: R&D expense over total
assets, with the tags named,

s10-101
00:06:02.685 --> 00:06:05.922
how the quarters field is handled,
and a filter to 10-Ks.

s10-102
00:06:05.972 --> 00:06:09.155
Aggregation: the median
by two-digit SIC and year.

s10-103
00:06:09.205 --> 00:06:14.595
Output: the figure and the CSV,
both named before anything runs.

s10-104
00:06:14.645 --> 00:06:20.366
And the paper trail: named scripts only,
the data profile, DECISIONS, LOG,

s10-105
00:06:20.416 --> 00:06:23.530
and an attrition row every
time the sample size changes.

s11-106
00:06:24.280 --> 00:06:27.935
The first figure is almost
never right, and that's normal.

s11-107
00:06:27.985 --> 00:06:31.894
Don't answer that with one long
prompt that tries to fix everything.

s11-108
00:06:31.944 --> 00:06:34.127
Do small, targeted rounds.

s11-109
00:06:34.177 --> 00:06:36.425
Fix the axes and labels and look at it,

s11-110
00:06:36.475 --> 00:06:41.219
then fix the industry-bucket granularity
and look again. Count the rounds.

s11-111
00:06:41.269 --> 00:06:45.107
Two or three is typical, and if you're
past three and it isn't converging,

s11-112
00:06:45.157 --> 00:06:47.438
stop and rethink the request.

s11-113
00:06:47.488 --> 00:06:50.409
The tree on the slide is
for when output goes wrong.

s11-114
00:06:50.459 --> 00:06:53.090
If it's an execution
glitch, let it recover.

s11-115
00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:56.386
If the strategy is wrong,
press Escape and redirect.

s11-116
00:06:56.436 --> 00:06:59.730
And if a targeted redirect
hasn't fixed it within two turns,

s11-117
00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:03.350
stop and re-plan instead of
arguing with it for ten more.

s12-118
00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:08.345
Before you accept any computed
result, run at least two checks.

s12-119
00:07:08.395 --> 00:07:10.852
The first is a benchmark comparison.

s12-120
00:07:10.902 --> 00:07:13.798
Pick industries where you
already have a strong prior.

s12-121
00:07:13.848 --> 00:07:18.885
Pharma and tech should show clearly higher
R&D intensity than utilities or retail,

s12-122
00:07:18.935 --> 00:07:21.114
so check that the figure shows that.

s12-123
00:07:21.164 --> 00:07:24.751
The second is a plausibility
check tied to a known event.

s12-124
00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:28.298
Around 2020, the series may show some
response to the disruption of that year.

s12-125
00:07:28.348 --> 00:07:31.850
You don't need to know the
direction ahead of time.

s12-126
00:07:31.900 --> 00:07:35.950
But if the line is perfectly flat
through a period you know was turbulent,

s12-127
00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:37.284
check whether that's real,

s12-128
00:07:37.334 --> 00:07:41.745
because it can also mean something upstream
was over-aggregated or built wrong.

s12-129
00:07:41.795 --> 00:07:44.393
Name both checks before
the figure renders.

s12-130
00:07:44.443 --> 00:07:47.170
And before either one,
plot the raw series.

s13-131
00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.015
So before you compute a
median by industry and year,

s13-132
00:07:51.065 --> 00:07:55.893
plot the firm-level ratio itself,
every observation, by fiscal year.

s13-133
00:07:55.943 --> 00:07:59.054
Do it once before you
winsorize and once after.

s13-134
00:07:59.104 --> 00:08:01.582
A lot of times the
problems are right there.

s13-135
00:08:01.632 --> 00:08:05.171
A few firms reporting R&D
larger than their total assets.

s13-136
00:08:05.221 --> 00:08:07.991
A year where the number
of observations collapses.

s13-137
00:08:08.041 --> 00:08:11.634
A cluster of exact zeros that
should be missing values.

s13-138
00:08:11.684 --> 00:08:16.054
Each of those is obvious in a raw scatter,
and you'd never see it in a median.

s13-139
00:08:16.104 --> 00:08:18.981
The raw plot doesn't replace
the two named checks.

s13-140
00:08:19.031 --> 00:08:22.670
You use it to find data problems
before you summarize anything.

s14-141
00:08:23.420 --> 00:08:28.166
The common failure in this module is
treating a plausible-looking figure as a

s14-142
00:08:28.216 --> 00:08:28.976
verified one.

s14-143
00:08:29.026 --> 00:08:33.739
Clean axis labels, a sensible legend,
smooth lines... it looks right.

s14-144
00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.323
But none of that tells you whether
the tag selection, the filter,

s14-145
00:08:37.373 --> 00:08:38.595
or the join was right.

s14-146
00:08:38.645 --> 00:08:43.676
Claude Code can tell you the median R&D
intensity came out at thirty-one percent

s14-147
00:08:43.726 --> 00:08:46.950
of assets for SIC code 28 in fiscal 2022.

s14-148
00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:50.839
It can't tell you, on its own authority,
whether that's plausible. It is,

s14-149
00:08:50.889 --> 00:08:55.386
because SIC 28 holds hundreds of small
biotech filers with big R&D budgets

s14-150
00:08:55.436 --> 00:08:56.943
relative to their assets.

s14-151
00:08:56.993 --> 00:09:00.916
But knowing that is accounting
and finance judgment,

s14-152
00:09:00.966 --> 00:09:02.700
which you have to bring yourself.

s14-153
00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:05.290
So run the checks even
when the plot looks clean.

s15-154
00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:08.919
So the verification habit for this module:

s15-155
00:09:08.969 --> 00:09:12.527
decide your benchmark comparison and
your plausibility check before the figure

s15-156
00:09:12.577 --> 00:09:12.962
renders.

s15-157
00:09:13.012 --> 00:09:14.041
If you name them in advance,

s15-158
00:09:14.091 --> 00:09:18.845
you're less likely to look at whatever
the figure shows and reason backward to a

s15-159
00:09:18.895 --> 00:09:21.344
story that makes it look right.

s15-160
00:09:21.394 --> 00:09:24.674
Write both checks into your README,
with the source you're checking against,

s15-161
00:09:24.724 --> 00:09:27.670
so anyone reading the project
can see what you checked.

s16-162
00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.551
These are the same three mantras
from Module 1, applied to more work.

s16-163
00:09:32.601 --> 00:09:35.199
Files persist and context doesn't.

s16-164
00:09:35.249 --> 00:09:40.202
Here that means DECISIONS.md, LOG.md,
and attrition rows at every stage,

s16-165
00:09:40.252 --> 00:09:41.554
not just at the end.

s16-166
00:09:41.604 --> 00:09:42.380
Trust, but verify.

s16-167
00:09:42.430 --> 00:09:46.828
Here that means two named sanity
checks before you sign off,

s16-168
00:09:46.878 --> 00:09:52.311
instead of a general sense that the
figure looks fine. And be specific.

s16-169
00:09:52.361 --> 00:09:57.037
That's six parts now: data, join, measure,
aggregation, output, and paper trail.

s16-170
00:09:57.087 --> 00:10:01.228
A full pipeline has more decisions
in it than a single script,

s16-171
00:10:01.278 --> 00:10:03.730
and you state each one in the prompt.

s17-172
00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.275
Here's what autonomous
error recovery looks like.

s17-173
00:10:07.325 --> 00:10:10.427
This is a stylized transcript,
not a screenshot.

s17-174
00:10:10.477 --> 00:10:14.772
The prompt asks for the
first-quarter 2024 FSDS zip.

s17-175
00:10:14.822 --> 00:10:17.168
The download script runs and
gets a bare 403 Forbidden,

s17-176
00:10:17.218 --> 00:10:23.073
because the SEC rejects bulk-file
requests that don't identify who's asking.

s17-177
00:10:23.123 --> 00:10:27.708
Claude reads the error, adds a User-Agent
header with a name and an email address,

s17-178
00:10:27.758 --> 00:10:30.340
runs the script again,
and the file comes down,

s17-179
00:10:30.390 --> 00:10:32.818
without you typing any part of the fix.

s17-180
00:10:32.868 --> 00:10:34.650
So here you watch rather than drive,

s17-181
00:10:34.700 --> 00:10:37.710
and you step in only if
the strategy looks wrong.

s18-182
00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:42.721
In the live session, the demo runs
thirty-five minutes in five steps.

s18-183
00:10:42.771 --> 00:10:47.914
First I read the opening prompt aloud,
slowly, because it's the template you keep.

s18-184
00:10:47.964 --> 00:10:49.601
That takes about eight minutes.

s18-185
00:10:49.651 --> 00:10:53.432
Then the download and the
403 recovery, which we watch.

s18-186
00:10:53.482 --> 00:10:58.525
Then cleaning and the join on ADSH,
with the attrition rows printing as it goes.

s18-187
00:10:58.575 --> 00:11:01.758
Then the first-draft figure
and two rounds of refinement.

s18-188
00:11:01.808 --> 00:11:04.830
And last, the sanity checks,
the sign-off, and the README,

s18-189
00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:06.970
which gets written at the very end.

s19-190
00:11:07.720 --> 00:11:09.574
In the first half of the demo,

s19-191
00:11:09.624 --> 00:11:12.235
the six-part prompt goes up
on the screen and I read it.

s19-192
00:11:12.285 --> 00:11:13.886
I don't retype it.

s19-193
00:11:13.936 --> 00:11:15.414
Then the download,

s19-194
00:11:15.464 --> 00:11:19.984
where you may see the SEC's User-Agent
403 and the agent's own recovery.

s19-195
00:11:20.034 --> 00:11:21.742
Then the join.

s19-196
00:11:21.792 --> 00:11:25.702
Watch the row count in each file
before the join and the count after,

s19-197
00:11:25.752 --> 00:11:29.205
and any ADSH values that
get flagged as unmatched.

s19-198
00:11:29.255 --> 00:11:34.273
While the attrition rows print, I'll stop
and read one DECISIONS.md entry out loud,

s19-199
00:11:34.323 --> 00:11:38.264
for example which quarters value it
kept for assets versus R&D, and why,

s19-200
00:11:38.314 --> 00:11:42.390
so you see the paper trail being
written as the choices get made.

s20-201
00:11:43.140 --> 00:11:47.263
In the second half, the first draft
of the figure will render wrong,

s20-202
00:11:47.313 --> 00:11:48.576
and we count the rounds together.

s20-203
00:11:48.626 --> 00:11:51.210
Round one is axes and labels.

s20-204
00:11:51.260 --> 00:11:53.830
Round two is the
industry-bucket granularity,

s20-205
00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.643
for example cutting down to a readable
number of two-digit SIC lines.

s20-206
00:11:58.693 --> 00:12:02.629
Then I run both sanity checks
and say what each one is testing.

s20-207
00:12:02.679 --> 00:12:04.693
The README gets written
only after they pass,

s20-208
00:12:04.743 --> 00:12:08.130
so it describes a pipeline
that's already been verified.

s21-209
00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:12.257
Then the lab: eighty minutes,
one continuous block,

s21-210
00:12:12.307 --> 00:12:15.833
from an empty folder to a
verified figure and a README.

s21-211
00:12:15.883 --> 00:12:17.358
You choose one measure.

s21-212
00:12:17.408 --> 00:12:23.363
Base is book leverage, long-term debt over
total assets, by two-digit SIC and year.

s21-213
00:12:23.413 --> 00:12:28.289
Standard is the demo's R&D intensity,
but grouped by one-digit SIC.

s21-214
00:12:28.339 --> 00:12:30.522
Stretch is revenue concentration,

s21-215
00:12:30.572 --> 00:12:34.183
the share of total revenue from
the ten largest filers each year,

s21-216
00:12:34.233 --> 00:12:36.192
which takes more cleaning judgment.

s21-217
00:12:36.242 --> 00:12:41.550
Your measure is different from the demo's,
so the verification is your own.

s21-218
00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:47.126
Use the five quarters the starter pack pins:
the first quarter of 2013, 2016, 2019,

s21-219
00:12:47.176 --> 00:12:49.278
2022, and 2024.

s21-220
00:12:49.328 --> 00:12:51.658
And the requirements are the
same as the demo: named scripts,

s21-221
00:12:51.708 --> 00:12:57.070
DECISIONS and LOG requested upfront,
an attrition table, and one figure.

s22-222
00:12:57.820 --> 00:13:01.259
This is the checklist you work
through before you call the lab done.

s22-223
00:13:01.309 --> 00:13:05.252
It's self-graded, pass or fail,
and I spot-check a sample.

s22-224
00:13:05.302 --> 00:13:09.377
The pipeline reruns start to finish
from the README in a fresh clone.

s22-225
00:13:09.427 --> 00:13:14.859
Sub and num are joined on ADSH,
with row counts stated before and after.

s22-226
00:13:14.909 --> 00:13:20.148
Every change in sample size is in the
attrition table and explained in DECISIONS.md.

s22-227
00:13:20.198 --> 00:13:23.759
The data profile covers the row definition,
the uniqueness test, tag coverage,

s22-228
00:13:23.809 --> 00:13:26.155
and known problems.

s22-229
00:13:26.205 --> 00:13:30.511
Two sanity checks are written down with
sources, and the raw series is plotted.

s22-230
00:13:30.561 --> 00:13:32.170
And the figure comes from a script.

s23-231
00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:37.345
Now the WRDS checkpoint,
and this one is a hard gate.

s23-232
00:13:37.395 --> 00:13:40.359
By the end of this module
you submit one of two things.

s23-233
00:13:40.409 --> 00:13:43.583
Either a tunnel status screenshot
showing ssh wrds connecting,

s23-234
00:13:43.633 --> 00:13:46.923
the Duo push arriving,
and the connection succeeding.

s23-235
00:13:46.973 --> 00:13:50.767
Or a flag on your exit ticket
saying exactly what went wrong.

s23-236
00:13:50.817 --> 00:13:55.494
I triage those that night, and fixes
get announced the morning of Module 3.

s23-237
00:13:55.544 --> 00:13:59.086
Fixing an account or Duo
problem takes at least two days.

s23-238
00:13:59.136 --> 00:14:02.426
If your WRDS access isn't
working at this checkpoint,

s23-239
00:14:02.476 --> 00:14:07.568
you're provisionally on the public-data
fallback track for Module 4,

s23-240
00:14:07.618 --> 00:14:09.257
so you can plan for it now.

s23-241
00:14:09.307 --> 00:14:12.950
If you get it working before Module 4,
you can switch to the live track.

s24-242
00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:15.343
We close with a debrief.

s24-243
00:14:15.393 --> 00:14:18.025
Whose first-draft figure
was actually right?

s24-244
00:14:18.075 --> 00:14:20.734
Expect close to zero, and that's normal.

s24-245
00:14:20.784 --> 00:14:25.056
What did Claude recover from on its own,
and where did you have to step in?

s24-246
00:14:25.106 --> 00:14:30.120
Then the exit ticket: the standard three
questions, plus one sentence on sub-agents.

s24-247
00:14:30.170 --> 00:14:33.976
If you saw Claude start one during
your lab, what do you think it was for?

s24-248
00:14:34.026 --> 00:14:36.310
We answer that properly in Module 4.

s24-249
00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:39.624
And make sure your WRDS
checkpoint is submitted.

s24-250
00:14:39.674 --> 00:14:40.690
See you in the lab.
